# Settings for autopilot. Copy to autopilot.env (all projects) or <project>/.sdlc/autopilot.env (one project). Both are git-ignored.
# One NAME=value per line. The environment wins over files; the project file wins over this one.
# Builder/QA models are provider/model as listed by `opencode models`; the leader is a `claude --model` alias or id.

# LEADER_MODEL=sonnet
# BUILDER_MODEL=opencode/muse-spark-1.3-contributor-free
# QA_MODEL=opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash

# Visual review (off by default): after a feature is accepted, a second model that is NOT the one that wrote the tests looks at the
# screenshots against the spec and lists what looks wrong (visual-review.md, shown on diagrams/evidence.html). Suspicions only: it never
# changes the verdict and never starts a repair round. It costs one extra model call, so it is opt-in.
# The models must accept images (`opencode models --verbose`, capabilities.input.image). A comma list is a fallback chain, as for the builder.
# VISUAL_QA=1
# VISUAL_MODEL=opencode-go/glm-5.3-flash,opencode-go/mimo-v2.5,opencode-go/longcat-2.5-preview-free,opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash

# One lane can use another model (lane = slice id, upper-cased). Also spreads rate limits:
# BUILDER_MODEL_UI=opencode-go/another-model
# QA_MODEL_UI=opencode-go/another-model

# Builder and QA never wait for a human to click "Allow": 1 (default) allows every permission request, including
# access outside the project folder (e.g. /tmp). 0 makes opencode ask in its window again (the call then stalls).
# OC_AUTO=1

# Provider keys are NOT stored here: run `opencode auth login` (opencode) and `claude login` (leader),
# or export the provider's own variable (e.g. GEMINI_API_KEY, CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN) in your shell,
# or use the proxy's key files (~/.config/jev-cli/gemini_key.txt, cloudflare_key.txt, mode 600).

# Jev routing judgments (which backend the local jev-proxy talks to). Non-secrets only;
# tokens stay out of this file (see above). The environment wins over
# ~/.config/jev-cli/config.json, which wins over the proxy's built-in defaults.
# JEV_BACKEND=clef             # gemini | openai | clef
# JEV_MODEL=@cf/cloudflare/clef-flash   # gemini: gemini-2.5-flash
# JEV_CF_ACCOUNT_ID=abc123     # Cloudflare account id (not a secret)

# Whether the feature's own files under .sdlc/ (spec, plan, acceptance, ownership, e2e tests) are committed with the code.
# yes (default) commits them; no keeps the whole .sdlc/ folder out of git (it is added to .git/info/exclude, which is local to
# this clone and never committed) and leaves it on disk to read. Set it per project in <project>/.sdlc/autopilot.env.
# COMMIT_SDLC=no

# Branches autopilot never commits on or pushes. If the project is on one of these when a feature is accepted,
# the commit goes to autopilot/<feature> instead. Space or comma separated; default "main master".
# PROTECTED_BRANCHES=main dev test

# Issue tracker: lets the QA agent look up and file an issue for each product defect (off when unset).
# Choose the tracker PER PROJECT in <project>/.sdlc/autopilot.env (git-ignored), not in the shared file: one project may use
# GitHub, another Jira. GitHub uses the gh CLI's own login (`gh auth login`). For Jira, JIRA_EMAIL and JIRA_API_TOKEN
# (https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens) go in that same project file or your shell.
# ISSUE_TRACKER=github          # github | jira
# ISSUE_LABELS=autopilot        # optional; labels must already exist on GitHub
# ISSUE_GITHUB_REPO=owner/name  # optional; default is the repo of the current directory
# JIRA_BASE_URL=https://your-team.atlassian.net
# JIRA_PROJECT=ABC
# JIRA_ISSUE_TYPE=Bug
# JIRA_EMAIL=you@example.com
# JIRA_API_TOKEN=

# Deployed env that pct102-qa-verify tests when --env is not given (name only the one host it may test)
# QA_ENV_URL=https://my-app-git-dev.vercel.app/

# ---- QA loop (tools/sdlc-daily.mjs check|fix|verify; skills pct102-qa-lead-check, pct102-dev-lead-fix, pct102-qa-lead-verify). Put these in <project>/.sdlc/autopilot.env ----
# QA_URLS="/vi/about /vi/support"   # extra pages the crawl from QA_ENV_URL does not reach (paths or full URLs)
# AGENT_CMD="bash $HOME/AI_SKILLS/tools/oc-daily-agent.sh"   # opencode with the BUILDER_MODEL chain (next model when one hits its limit); or any command that takes the prompt as its LAST argument and may run git, gh and node
# PR_BASE=dev                       # fix PRs go here and only here are they auto-merged (never main)
# MAX_NEW_ISSUES=15  MAX_DEV_ISSUES=5  MAX_FIX_ATTEMPTS=2  QA_SCOUT_MAX_PAGES=25  AGENT_TIMEOUT_MIN=30
# MERGE_MAX_FILES=8  MERGE_MAX_LINES=300  MERGE_WAIT_MIN=10  MERGE_REQUIRE_CI=1  # MERGE_BLOCK_PATHS=<regex>
# DEPLOY_WAIT_MIN=15  DEPLOY_SETTLE_SEC=20  CONFLICT_WAIT_SEC=60   # fix: wait for the dev deploy after each merge, then verify that issue before the next fix
# HEARTBEAT_SEC=60  VERIFY_RERUN=1  VERIFY_MAX_PAGES=8  VERIFY_PARALLEL=3  VERIFY_SWEEP=1  VERIFY_TIMEOUT_MIN=15  RERUN_TIMEOUT_MIN=10   # fix/verify: progress line every N s; ui-qa re-runs done by the script (pages, parallel runs, sweep); agent time for the verdict
# DAILY_NOTIFY=0                    # silence the macOS notification at the end of a stage
# Design rules (Impeccable, third-party via npx, ~50 s per page) run in check and verify; 0 turns them off
# QA_IMPECCABLE=1
# Low design rules become issues too, one per rule (labels design-polish, priority:low); 0 keeps them in the report only
# QA_DESIGN_POLISH=1
# devfix order: priority (default: low impact first, then oldest) or oldest
# DEVFIX_ORDER=priority
# Verify also re-tests this many pages that the fix's changed files reach but the issue does not list
# VERIFY_IMPACT_PAGES=4

# --- Roles tested logged in (profiles). Each profile = a saved login file + the pages that role sees.
# QA_PROFILES="admin student1 student3 devadmin"
# QA_LOCAL_URL=http://localhost:3000          # the project's own server (start it first: DB up, `npm run dev`, test users seeded)
# QA_PROFILE_ADMIN_AUTH=playwright/.auth/user.json          # saved logins the project's Playwright setup already writes
# QA_PROFILE_ADMIN_PATHS="/vi/admin /vi/question-management"
# QA_PROFILE_STUDENT1_AUTH=playwright/.auth/student-g1.json
# QA_PROFILE_STUDENT1_PATHS="/vi/home /vi/math"
# A deployed environment with a real sign-in (Google): create the file once with
#   node ~/AI_SKILLS/tools/ui-qa.mjs https://<dev-url>/vi --login ~/.config/ui-qa/dev-admin.json
# QA_PROFILE_DEVADMIN_AUTH=~/.config/ui-qa/dev-admin.json
# QA_PROFILE_DEVADMIN_URL=https://<dev-url>
# QA_PROFILE_DEVADMIN_PATHS="/vi/home /vi/admin"
# Login files are passwords: keep them out of git. A login that expires makes that profile fail with a clear message.
# A deployed profile's file is renewed from the dedicated Chrome profile when it is older than LOGIN_MAX_AGE_DAYS (default 2). A file made some
# other way (for example a test user's session your project mints; a real Google sign-in is not needed for it) must not be overwritten by that:
# add _RENEW=0 to that profile. It then runs until the file expires and is skipped with "no saved login" after that.
# QA_PROFILE_DEVSTUDENT1_AUTH=playwright/.auth/dev-student-g1.json
# QA_PROFILE_DEVSTUDENT1_URL=https://<dev-url>
# QA_PROFILE_DEVSTUDENT1_PATHS="/vi/home /vi/math"
# QA_PROFILE_DEVSTUDENT1_RENEW=0
# pct102-qa-verify reads these profiles too: a login wall on the deployed env is passed with the profile whose _URL is that env. pct102-dev-fix does the same
# for the local app (a profile whose _URL is localhost or unset).
# ui-qa never answers a quiz or clicks through a flow, so states behind interaction (feedback after an answer, a result page) need the project's own
# command (read by pct102-qa-verify and pct102-dev-fix). Set it once; {url} and {auth} are replaced by the base URL and the profile's login file; it must write
# screenshots and a report.json:
# QA_PLAY_CMD="npx dotenv -e .env.local -- node scripts/qa/play-lesson.mjs --url {url} --auth {auth}"
# qaverify (sdlc-daily) runs QA_PLAY_CMD itself, as the issue's QA profile, when the issue text or pages match QA_PLAY_WHEN (a regex; unset = never). It appends
# --out <issue dir>/play (or play-1, play-2 ...) and the agent gets those screenshots + report.json as evidence. QA_PLAY_VARIANTS runs it once per variant ("|" separated).
# QA_PLAY_WHEN="lesson|quiz"
# {subject} in QA_PLAY_CMD becomes vietnamese when the issue text or pages mention it, else math (so a Vietnamese issue is not played with a math lesson):
# QA_PLAY_CMD="npx dotenv -e .env.local -- node scripts/qa/play-lesson.mjs --url {url} --auth {auth} --subject {subject}"
# Features / enhancements are proven by running the new flow, not by a crawl. QA_FEATURE_PROFILE is the test role (e.g. a local student) used when a feature issue has no
# "QA profile:" line. QA_FEATURE_CMD is run by qaverify itself: {url} {auth} {out} (also $QA_OUT); it must write PNGs of the new behaviour into {out}. QA_FEATURE_WHEN is a regex
# on the issue title+labels+body picking the issues it applies to (unset = every feature issue). One pair serves one subject: for several subjects number them,
# QA_FEATURE_1_WHEN/CMD, QA_FEATURE_2_WHEN/CMD ... up to 9; the first whose WHEN matches wins, none matching = the agent writes the script. The agent is told to attach those screenshots and never the home page.
# QA_FEATURE_PROFILE=student1
# QA_FEATURE_CMD="PLAYWRIGHT_HTML_OPEN=never npx playwright test tests/e2e/my-feature.spec.ts --project=chromium-student-g1"
# QA_FEATURE_WHEN="REORDER|IMAGE_PICK"
# qacheck scope: `qacheck --profile student1,student3 [--paths "/vi/vietnamese /vi/learn"]` crawls only those roles (and skips the landing crawl) instead of every QA_PROFILES entry.

# QA_PLAY_VARIANTS="--scheme light|--scheme dark"
